$ rgit init
Initialized empty git repository in .git/
$ write a.txt (5 bytes)
$ rgit add a.txt
Staged files to index (1 new).
$ rgit commit -m base
[main (root-commit) <H1:7>] base
 Author: Test Author <author@example.com>
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)
 create mode 100644 a.txt
$ rgit switch -c feat
Switched to a new branch 'feat'
$ write a.txt (5 bytes)
$ rgit commit -a -m feat
[feat <H2:7>] feat
 Author: Test Author <author@example.com>
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)
$ rgit switch main
Switched to branch 'main'
$ write a.txt (5 bytes)
$ rgit commit -a -m main
[main <H3:7>] main
 Author: Test Author <author@example.com>
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)
$ rgit merge feat
Auto-merging a.txt
CONFLICT (content): Merge conflict in a.txt
Automatic merge failed; fix conflicts and then commit the result.
[exit 1]
$ rgit oops
Most likely fixes, best guess first:

  1) Cancel the merge that's in progress
     A merge stopped halfway (usually on conflicts); cancelling it puts every file back the way
     it was before the merge. To finish it instead: `rgit conflicts`.
     $ rgit merge --abort

  2) Undo your last command: `rgit merge feat` (just now)
     Puts back what it changed: files restored: a.txt; index (staging area): put back as it was;
     the in-progress merge is cancelled (its state files are removed).
     $ rgit undo

Run one with `rgit oops <number>` (it can be taken back with `rgit undo`).
$ rgit oops 1
Fix 1: Cancel the merge that's in progress
$ rgit merge --abort
Done. `rgit undo` takes it back.
$ rgit status --short
$ rgit switch --detach main
HEAD is now at <H3:7> (detached)
[stderr]
warning: You are in a detached HEAD state.
  You can look around, make experimental commits, or switch -c <branch> to keep them.
$ write d.txt (2 bytes)
$ rgit add d.txt
Staged files to index (1 new).
$ rgit commit -m experiment
[detached HEAD <H4:7>] experiment
 Author: Test Author <author@example.com>
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)
 create mode 100644 d.txt
$ rgit oops
Most likely fixes, best guess first:

  1) Keep the 1 commit you made on a detached HEAD on a new branch 'experiment'
     HEAD isn't on a branch, so switching away would leave these commits behind; this gives them
     a branch and puts you on it.
     $ rgit switch -c experiment

  2) Undo your last command: `rgit commit -m experiment` (just now)
     Puts back what it changed: HEAD: detached at <H4:7> -> detached at <H3:7>.
     $ rgit undo

Run one with `rgit oops <number>` (it can be taken back with `rgit undo`).

